# Europe batch (1930-1947): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"d-depression1930": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Heinrich Brüning set out to ride out the Depression by deflation, defending the currency and the reparations settlement while American credit drained away. Unemployment rose from 1.3 million in 1929 to 6 million by early 1932 and the Reichstag was bypassed by decree. The slump turned the Nazi party from a 2.6 per cent fringe into the second party in Germany within a year and hollowed out parliamentary government before Hitler touched it.",
 now="Germany's political collapse was deflationary, in 1930-32, which is why German policy since 1949 fears both inflation and mass unemployment and why the European Central Bank's mandate was written the way it was.",
 story="Germany's recovery after 1924 ran on short-term American credit. After the 1929 crash the loans were called in; unemployment rose from 1.3 million in 1929 to 3 million by September 1930 and 6 million by early 1932. German Chancellor Heinrich Brüning governed by presidential decree from July 1930 with the backing of President Paul von Hindenburg and cut wages and benefits to defend the currency. On 14 September 1930 the NSDAP took 18.3 per cent of the vote, up from 2.6 per cent in 1928.",
 why="German recovery after the 1924 Dawes Plan was financed by short-term loans from New York, and when the crash called them in German banks and employers had no reserve. Brüning's answer, deflation by presidential decree, deepened the slump to protect the currency and the reparations settlement.",
 trig="Unemployment passed 3 million in September 1930; on 14 September the NSDAP won 107 seats, up from 12.",
 then="Deflation continued into 1932 and unemployment reached 6 million. The Reichstag was bypassed by decree. The NSDAP took 37.3 per cent in July 1932.",
 fact="Brüning earned the nickname 'Hunger Chancellor'; he later said he had been a hundred metres from the finish when Hindenburg dismissed him in May 1932."),
"d-holodomor": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin set out to collectivise farming and pay for industry with grain, and to break Ukrainian peasant resistance and the nationalism he suspected behind it. Grain quotas and a sealed border starved millions of Ukrainians to death in 1932-33 while exports continued. The famine broke the Ukrainian countryside as a political force, and the denial that followed became a foundation of Ukrainian national identity against Moscow.",
 now="Ukraine and many other countries recognise the Holodomor as a genocide; Russia denies that Ukrainians were singled out. The dispute is one of the memory conflicts behind the war since 2014.",
 story="Forced collectivisation and impossible grain quotas brought famine across the USSR in 1932-33. In Ukraine and the Kuban the government of Soviet leader Joseph Stalin blacklisted villages that missed their quotas, stopped starving peasants from leaving to look for food and kept exporting grain. Millions died.",
 why="Stalin's drive to collectivise farming and pay for industry came first. His suspicion of Ukrainian nationalism directed the harshest measures at Ukraine and the Kuban.",
 then="The Soviet state denied the famine for decades and punished those who spoke of it. Ukraine's 2006 law declared it a genocide, and more than 20 states have followed; Russia's position is that it was a famine shared across the USSR.",
 fact="Welsh journalist Gareth Jones reported the famine in 1933 after walking through Ukrainian villages; Moscow correspondents, led by Walter Duranty of the New York Times, denied it in print."),
"eu193391": dict(
 bluf="The conservatives around German President Paul von Hindenburg appointed Nazi leader Adolf Hitler chancellor to harness his mass following for their own authoritarian aims. Hitler used the office to dismantle German democracy within months, and the men who meant to control him were sidelined or killed. The regime that resulted made a European war and the murder of Europe's Jews its policy, and its rise is the defining case of a democracy dismantled from inside.",
 now="It is the standard case of a democracy dismantled from within by legal means, and the reason the Federal Republic's constitution of 1949 bars parties that work against the democratic order.",
 story="The Depression and anger at Versailles had made the Nazi Party the largest in the Reichstag by 1932. Former Chancellor Franz von Papen and other conservatives around German President Paul von Hindenburg persuaded him to appoint Nazi leader Adolf Hitler chancellor on 30 January 1933 in a cabinet with only two other Nazis. The Reichstag fire of 27 February gave Hitler a decree suspending civil liberties, and the Enabling Act of 23 March let his cabinet make laws alone.",
 why="The Depression had put six million out of work and polarised a parliament that could no longer govern. Fear of communism and anger at Versailles gave Hitler his vote. A conservative right willing to use him gave him the office.",
 trig="Conservative politicians around President Hindenburg persuaded him to appoint Hitler chancellor on 30 January 1933.",
 then="The Reichstag fire decree and the Enabling Act gave Hitler dictatorial power by March 1933. Other parties, trade unions and the free press were abolished by July, and Jews were stripped of their rights from April. Germany rearmed and pursued expansion that ended in war in 1939.",
 myth="Hitler was swept to power by a majority of Germans. The Nazis took 33 per cent in the last free election of November 1932, down from 37 per cent in July, and Hitler got the chancellorship by appointment from a president who despised him."),
"w2-reichstag": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler used the burning of the Reichstag to claim a communist uprising and win emergency powers before the election a week away. President Paul von Hindenburg signed a decree the next day suspending civil liberties, and the police arrested thousands of communists. The decree stayed in force until 1945 and is the standard case of a leader using an emergency to end a constitution.",
 now="The fire gave Hitler the emergency decree that suspended civil liberties four weeks after he took office; the arrests of Communists under it cleared the way for the Enabling Act and one-party rule within a month.",
 story="On the night of 27 February 1933, four weeks after German Chancellor Adolf Hitler took office, the Reichstag building in Berlin burned. Dutch council communist Marinus van der Lubbe was arrested at the scene and said he had acted alone. Hitler blamed a communist plot, and an emergency decree signed by President Paul von Hindenburg on 28 February suspended freedom of speech, press and assembly. Thousands of communists were arrested before the election of 5 March.",
 why="Hitler wanted dictatorial power before his opponents could organise and before an election whose result he doubted. The fire supplied the emergency.",
 then="The Nazis and their allies won a majority on 5 March 1933 with the communist deputies in custody. The Enabling Act followed on 23 March.",
 myth="The Nazis set the fire themselves. Van der Lubbe's sole responsibility was accepted by the Nazi-era court and remains the reading with the most evidence; the question stays open because the regime destroyed what it could.",
 fact="Van der Lubbe was beheaded in January 1934 under a law passed after the fire and applied to him retroactively; a German court quashed his conviction in 2008."),
"w2-reichstag-b": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler asked the Reichstag to vote its own powers to his cabinet so that he could rule without further elections or decrees from the president. With communist deputies in custody and SA men in the hall, every party except the Social Democrats voted yes. Parliamentary government in Germany ended in one vote, and the Act is the case every postwar constitution was written to make impossible.",
 now="The Act let the cabinet legislate without the Reichstag, which ended parliamentary government in Germany in a single vote; the postwar constitution was written to make such a vote impossible.",
 story="The Reichstag met in the Kroll Opera House in Berlin on 23 March 1933, with its communist deputies arrested and SA men lining the hall. German Chancellor Adolf Hitler asked for a law giving his cabinet power to make laws without parliament for four years. It passed by 444 votes to 94. Only the Social Democrats, led by Otto Wels, voted against.",
 why="Hitler wanted a legal basis for dictatorship independent of the president's decrees. The Catholic Centre Party and others voted yes in return for promises Hitler did not keep.",
 then="Trade unions were abolished in May 1933 and the remaining parties banned or dissolved by July, which left Germany a one-party state. The Act was renewed in 1937 and 1939 and stayed in force until 1945.",
 fact="Otto Wels told Hitler to his face that 'you can take our lives and our freedom, but not our honour'; he died in exile in Paris in 1939."),
"w2-longknives": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler decided to destroy the SA leadership to win the army's loyalty before President Paul von Hindenburg died. The SS shot SA chief Ernst Röhm and dozens of others, along with old enemies, over three days in June and July 1934. The army accepted murder as policy, and when Hindenburg died a month later it swore an oath to Hitler personally.",
 now="The purge showed that the regime would murder openly and that the courts and the army would approve, which removed the last check inside Germany on Hitler's power.",
 story="The SA, the Nazis' brown-shirted street army of some three million men, wanted a 'second revolution' and its chief, Ernst Röhm, wanted it merged with the army. The army's generals saw it as a threat to their monopoly of arms. From 30 June to 2 July 1934 German Chancellor Adolf Hitler had the SS under Heinrich Himmler kill Röhm, the SA leadership and old enemies including former Chancellor Kurt von Schleicher. The dead numbered at least 85 by the regime's count and several hundred by other reckonings.",
 why="The SA threatened the army's monopoly of arms, and Hitler needed the army's loyalty before President Hindenburg died. The purge also removed rivals and settled old scores.",
 then="Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934. Hitler merged the offices of president and chancellor as Führer and the army swore an oath of loyalty to him by name. A law of 3 July declared the killings legal.",
 fact="Röhm was offered a pistol in his cell to shoot himself and refused; two SS officers shot him."),
"w2-nuremberg": dict(
 bluf="The Nazi government set out to write its racial doctrine into law and to answer party radicals demanding action against Jews. The Nuremberg Laws of September 1935 stripped German Jews of citizenship and banned marriage and sex between Jews and other Germans, with Jewishness defined by grandparents. Discrimination became a legal category of the state, the step on which every later measure against Jews rested.",
 now="The laws made ancestry the legal basis of citizenship, the first step in law from discrimination to murder, and they are the reference case for every later debate over citizenship defined by descent.",
 story="Announced at the Nazi party rally in Nuremberg on 15 September 1935, the Reich Citizenship Law made Jews 'subjects' rather than citizens, and the Law for the Protection of German Blood banned marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans. A decree in November defined who counted as Jewish by the religion of a person's grandparents, so that converts to Christianity and their children were caught too.",
 why="Nazi ideology held Jews to be a racial threat to the German nation. Party radicals were demanding action after a wave of street attacks on Jews that summer.",
 then="Persecution intensified through the 1930s as Jews were driven from the professions, schools and the economy. By 1938 about a quarter of Germany's Jews had emigrated.",
 fact="The laws were drafted in two days by officials summoned to Nuremberg for the rally, and the first text was written on the back of a menu card."),
"eu193692": dict(
 bluf="Spain's generals rose to overthrow an elected Republic they saw as the enemy of the Church, the army and property; the Republic fought to survive. Germany and Italy armed General Francisco Franco, the Soviet Union armed the Republic, and Franco won after three years and perhaps half a million dead. Spain got 36 years of dictatorship, and the war became the rehearsal ground for the weapons and alliances of the Second World War.",
 now="Franco's victory kept Spain out of the Second World War and under dictatorship until 1975, and the war fixed the left-right line that Spanish politics still runs along, as the exhumation of Franco's remains in 2019 showed.",
 story="Spanish generals rose against the elected Republic on 17-18 July 1936 and took about half the country; General Francisco Franco emerged as their leader. Germany and Italy sent aircraft, tanks and troops to Franco; the USSR sent arms to the Republic; volunteers came from across Europe and the Americas in the International Brigades. Madrid held until March 1939.",
 why="Spain was polarised between left and right over land, the Church, regional autonomy and the army. The coup of July 1936 failed to take the whole country, which turned it into a war.",
 trig="The military rising of 17-18 July 1936.",
 then="Franco ruled until 1975. German bombers tested their tactics on Guernica in April 1937. Britain and France kept to non-intervention while the Axis powers did not.",
 fact="Picasso's 'Guernica' hung in New York until 1981 on his instruction that it return only when Spain was again a republic or democracy."),
"w2-rhineland": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler sent troops into the demilitarised Rhineland to break the Versailles treaty and secure Germany's western border before expanding east. France, in an election campaign and unwilling to act without Britain, did nothing, and Britain saw no reason to fight over Germans entering Germany. Hitler concluded that the democracies would not fight, and the fortifications he built made France's alliances in eastern Europe hard to honour.",
 now="It is cited as the last cheap chance to stop Hitler, and the case invoked whenever leaders debate whether to answer a small breach of a treaty or border.",
 story="Versailles and the Locarno treaties banned German troops from the Rhineland, next to France. On 7 March 1936 German Chancellor Adolf Hitler sent about 3,000 men into the zone with orders to withdraw if France resisted. France consulted Britain and took no action.",
 why="Hitler wanted to break Versailles and secure Germany's western border before expanding east. He gambled that France, in an election campaign and unwilling to act without Britain, would not respond.",
 then="Germany began fortifying the Rhineland, which made it harder for France to help its eastern allies. The Rome-Berlin Axis with Italy followed in October 1936.",
 fact="Hitler later said the 48 hours after the march were the most nerve-racking of his life; a French response would have forced a retreat."),
"d-purge": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin set out to destroy every possible rival and every imagined 'enemy of the people' in the party, the army and the population. The NKVD under Nikolai Yezhov arrested more than 1.5 million people in 1936-38 and shot about 680,000. Terror became the Soviet state's governing method, and the Red Army lost most of its high command three years before the German invasion.",
 now="The purge killed about 700,000, destroyed the army's command before the German invasion, and made terror the Soviet state's governing method; every later Soviet leader had been complicit, which shaped the system's refusal to reckon with it.",
 story="In 1936-38 show trials in Moscow convicted old Bolshevik leaders of absurd plots, while the NKVD under Nikolai Yezhov arrested over 1.5 million people on the orders of Soviet leader Joseph Stalin. About 680,000 were shot and many more sent to Gulag labour camps. Three of five marshals and most of the army's senior officers were purged.",
 why="Stalin's drive to eliminate any possible rival or 'enemy of the people' came first. Quotas handed down to regional NKVD offices turned it into mass killing.",
 then="The weakened Red Army performed badly against Finland in 1939-40 and Germany in 1941. Yezhov was shot in 1940 and the terror blamed on him.",
 fact="Memorial, the group that documented the victims, was shut down by Russian courts in 2021."),
"eu193893": dict(
 bluf="British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain and French Premier Édouard Daladier set out to avoid a war over the Sudetenland that they were unready to fight. They gave German Chancellor Adolf Hitler the German-speaking border regions of Czechoslovakia, with its fortifications, without consulting Prague. Hitler took the rest of the country six months later, and 'Munich' became the name for the danger of conceding to an aggressor.",
 now="'Munich' is invoked in almost every debate about confronting aggression, sometimes misleadingly.",
 story="At Munich on 29-30 September 1938 British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain and French Premier Édouard Daladier agreed with German Chancellor Adolf Hitler and Italian leader Benito Mussolini that Germany could annex the Sudetenland, the German-speaking border regions of Czechoslovakia, within ten days. Czechoslovakia, which had an alliance with France and strong fortifications along that border, was not invited. Chamberlain flew home promising 'peace for our time'.",
 why="Britain and France were unready to fight and haunted by the last war. Many in both countries believed Germany had legitimate grievances over German-speaking areas.",
 then="Hitler took the rest of Czechoslovakia in March 1939 and invaded Poland in September. Britain and France rearmed faster after Munich than before it.",
 myth="Munich simply bought Britain a year to rearm. The year also gave Germany the Czech army's equipment and the Škoda works, and the balance of the twelve months is argued both ways.",
 fact="Chamberlain returned waving the paper and promising 'peace for our time'. War came 11 months later."),
"w2-anschluss": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler set out to bring Austria into the Reich, by pressure if he could and by force if he had to. Austrian Chancellor Kurt Schuschnigg called a plebiscite to resist, was forced to resign, and German troops entered on 12 March 1938 without resistance and to cheering crowds. Germany gained seven million people and the position to surround Czechoslovakia, and the absence of any Western response set up the Sudeten crisis.",
 now="Austria long called itself 'Hitler's first victim', a claim it revised from the 1980s to acknowledge Austrians' share in the regime.",
 story="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler, Austrian-born, pressured Austrian Chancellor Kurt Schuschnigg in February 1938 to admit Nazis to his government. Schuschnigg called a plebiscite on independence for 13 March; Hitler demanded its cancellation and his resignation. German troops entered Austria on 12 March 1938 without resistance, and a staged vote in April recorded 99 per cent for union.",
 why="Hitler wanted all German speakers in one Reich and Austria's gold, iron and manpower. Many Austrians supported union, and Italy, Austria's former protector, had moved into Germany's camp.",
 then="Vienna's Jews were attacked and humiliated in the streets within days, and the Nuremberg Laws applied from May. Hitler turned at once to the Sudetenland in Czechoslovakia, now surrounded by German territory on three sides.",
 fact="Schuschnigg spent the war in Nazi custody, including Dachau; he later taught in the United States."),
"w2-kristallnacht": dict(
 bluf="The Nazi leadership used the shooting of a German diplomat in Paris to launch a state-organised pogrom meant to drive Jews out of Germany and seize their property. SA and Hitler Youth burned over 1,000 synagogues, wrecked shops and homes, killed about 90 Jews and sent some 30,000 to concentration camps on 9-10 November 1938. Persecution by law became persecution by violence, and few countries opened their doors to the refugees it produced.",
 now="The pogrom was the turn from persecution by law to persecution by violence on the road to the Holocaust, and the world's refusal to take the refugees it produced is the reference case for every later refugee debate.",
 story="On 7 November 1938 Polish Jewish teenager Herschel Grynszpan shot German diplomat Ernst vom Rath in Paris. When vom Rath died on 9 November, Propaganda Minister Joseph Goebbels gave the signal and SA and Hitler Youth attacked Jews across Germany and Austria on the night of 9-10 November. Over 1,000 synagogues were burned or damaged, about 90 Jews were killed and some 30,000 Jewish men were sent to concentration camps. Jews were then fined 1 billion Reichsmarks for the damage.",
 why="The Nazi leadership used the shooting as a pretext to drive Jews out of Germany and seize their property, after years of escalating legal persecution.",
 then="Jews were excluded from schools and the economy. Many fled, and few countries took them.",
 fact="The name Kristallnacht, 'crystal night', refers to the broken glass; the insurance payouts for the glass alone went to the state."),
"eu193994": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler invaded Poland to win living space in the east and racial domination, after securing a deal with Soviet leader Joseph Stalin to divide eastern Europe. Six years of war killed 70-85 million people, including six million Jews in the Holocaust, and ended with Germany and Japan in ruins. The United States and the Soviet Union emerged as superpowers, Europe was divided between them, the United Nations was founded, and the European empires began to dissolve.",
 now="The postwar world, from the UN and NATO to the Cold War and decolonisation, grew from its ruins.",
 story="Having secured a secret deal with Soviet leader Joseph Stalin to divide eastern Europe, German Chancellor Adolf Hitler invaded Poland on 1 September 1939. Britain and France declared war two days later. Over the next six years the war spread to western Europe, the Soviet Union, North Africa, the Atlantic and, after Japan's attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941, the Pacific.",
 why="Hitler's goals of 'living space' in the east and racial domination came first. The failure of the Versailles settlement, the Depression and the democracies' appeasement gave him his opening, and Japanese and Italian expansion widened the war.",
 trig="Germany's invasion of Poland on 1 September 1939.",
 then="Germany surrendered in May 1945 and Japan in August. The Holocaust murdered six million Jews and millions of others. Europe was divided between the US and Soviet spheres, nuclear weapons were used, and the European empires began to dissolve.",
 fact="Of the 70-85 million dead, a majority were civilians; the Soviet Union alone lost about 27 million."),
"w2-pact": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler wanted to invade Poland without a two-front war; Soviet leader Joseph Stalin wanted time to rearm and territory on his western border. Their foreign ministers signed a non-aggression pact in Moscow on 23 August 1939 with a secret protocol dividing eastern Europe between them. Germany invaded Poland nine days later, the Soviet Union took eastern Poland, the Baltic states and Bessarabia, and the pact's memory still divides Russia from its western neighbours.",
 now="Its secret protocol still shapes Baltic and Polish memory of the war, and Russia's defence of the pact is a sore point in its relations with its western neighbours.",
 story="On 23 August 1939 German Foreign Minister Joachim von Ribbentrop and Soviet Foreign Minister Vyacheslav Molotov signed a ten-year non-aggression pact in Moscow, stunning a world that saw the two states as ideological enemies. A secret protocol, whose existence the USSR denied until 1989, divided eastern Europe: Germany would take western Poland; the Soviets eastern Poland, Latvia, Estonia, Finland and Bessarabia, with Lithuania added a month later.",
 why="Hitler wanted to avoid a two-front war. Stalin wanted time and territory, and had concluded that Britain and France would not fight for him.",
 then="Germany invaded Poland nine days later. The USSR invaded from the east on 17 September 1939 and annexed the Baltic states in 1940.",
 fact="Stalin toasted Hitler's health at the signing; the Soviet parliament condemned the secret protocol as void in December 1989."),
"w2-prague": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler set out to take the rest of Czechoslovakia for its industry and arms and to remove a defensible state from his path. He bullied Czech President Emil Hácha into 'requesting' German protection and marched into Prague on 15 March 1939. The Munich deal stood exposed as a sham, Britain and France guaranteed Poland, and the line that brought war in September was drawn.",
 now="It is the moment appeasement was discredited, and 'Munich' became the standard argument against conceding territory to an aggressor.",
 story="On 15 March 1939, after German Chancellor Adolf Hitler bullied Czechoslovakia's elderly president, Emil Hácha, into 'requesting' German protection during an all-night meeting in Berlin, German troops marched into Prague. Bohemia and Moravia became a German 'protectorate'. Slovakia, which had declared independence the day before under German pressure, became a client state, and Hungary seized Carpathian Ruthenia.",
 why="Hitler wanted Czech industry and arms and the removal of a defensible state from his path. The occupation of non-German lands showed his aims went beyond uniting German speakers.",
 then="Britain and France guaranteed Poland's independence on 31 March 1939. Hitler turned to Poland anyway.",
 fact="Hácha collapsed during the night meeting and was revived by Hitler's doctor to sign; Czech tanks taken in Prague equipped a quarter of the German armour that invaded France in 1940."),
"w2-winterwar": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin wanted a buffer around Leningrad and invaded Finland when it refused to cede the land. Finland's army held the Red Army for three months at a cost far below its own, then gave up about a tenth of its territory and kept its independence. The Red Army's showing convinced German Chancellor Adolf Hitler that the USSR could be beaten quickly, and it gave Finland the defence tradition that carried it into NATO in 2023.",
 now="It is a source of Finnish national pride and shaped its strong defence tradition; after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine, Finland joined NATO in 2023.",
 story="When Finland refused to cede land near Leningrad, the Soviet Union invaded on 30 November 1939, expecting victory within weeks. Outnumbered Finnish troops under Marshal Carl Gustaf Mannerheim, fighting on skis in temperatures far below freezing, destroyed Soviet columns in the forests, and the League of Nations expelled the USSR. Weight of numbers broke the Finnish defences on the Karelian Isthmus in February 1940, and in March Finland ceded about a tenth of its territory but kept its independence.",
 why="Stalin wanted a buffer zone around Leningrad and demanded Finnish territory, which Finland refused. The purge had left the Red Army without experienced commanders.",
 then="The Red Army's poor showing convinced Hitler the USSR would be easy to defeat, while Stalin began reforming his army. Finland joined Germany's attack on the USSR in 1941.",
 fact="The Molotov cocktail got its name in this war; Finnish troops named the petrol bomb after the Soviet foreign minister."),
"eu194095": dict(
 bluf="Germany set out to knock France out of the war in one campaign by striking through the Ardennes rather than through Belgium as in 1914. The thrust reached the Channel in ten days, trapped the Allied armies, and France signed an armistice on 22 June 1940, six weeks after the attack began. Britain stood alone, the Vichy regime collaborated with the occupier, and the defeat shaped French politics and the drive for European unity for the rest of the century.",
 now="France's defeat and collaboration shaped its postwar politics and its determination, with Germany, to build European unity.",
 story="On 10 May 1940 Germany invaded the Netherlands and Belgium. The main thrust came through the wooded Ardennes, bypassing France's Maginot Line; German tanks reached the Channel in ten days, trapping the Allied armies, of which over 338,000 men were evacuated from Dunkirk. Paris fell on 14 June, and France signed an armistice on 22 June.",
 why="German tactics of concentrated tanks and aircraft coordinated by radio beat a French army spread along a static line. French defensive doctrine and command failures left no reserve to meet the breakthrough.",
 then="France was split between German occupation and the collaborationist Vichy regime under Marshal Philippe Pétain. Britain, under Prime Minister Winston Churchill, fought on, and General Charles de Gaulle launched the Free French from London.",
 fact="Hitler had the 1940 armistice signed in the same railway carriage at Compiègne used for the German surrender of 1918, then took the carriage to Berlin."),
"w2-britain": dict(
 bluf="The Luftwaffe set out to destroy the Royal Air Force so that Germany could invade across the Channel; the RAF set out to deny it. Radar, a central control system and the switch of German bombing from airfields to London in September 1940 let Fighter Command hold, and Hitler postponed the invasion indefinitely on 17 September. Britain stayed in the war as the base from which western Europe was later liberated.",
 now="Britain's survival kept a base in western Europe for the later American and British return, and radar-guided integrated air defence remains the model for modern air defence.",
 story="In summer 1940 the Luftwaffe under Hermann Göring set out to destroy the Royal Air Force as a prelude to invasion. RAF Fighter Command under Air Chief Marshal Hugh Dowding, guided by a chain of radar stations and a central control system, inflicted heavy losses. In September Germany switched from attacking airfields to bombing London, which gave the RAF time to recover, and on 17 September Hitler postponed the invasion indefinitely.",
 why="Britain refused to make peace after France fell, and Hitler needed control of the air before he could invade across the Channel.",
 then="Germany's bombing of cities, the Blitz, killed about 43,000 British civilians by May 1941. Hitler turned to planning the invasion of the Soviet Union.",
 fact="About a fifth of Fighter Command's pilots were from outside Britain; the Polish 303 Squadron claimed the highest score of any squadron in the battle."),
"w2-dunkirk": dict(
 bluf="Britain set out to save its army after German tanks reached the Channel and cut the Allied forces in two. A German pause, the Royal Navy and hundreds of small civilian boats brought over 338,000 British and French troops off the beaches between 26 May and 4 June 1940. Britain kept an army with which to fight on, and a defeat became the national story of resilience.",
 now="Saving the army let Britain fight on, keeping a base from which Europe was later liberated; it shows that avoiding total defeat can matter as much as winning.",
 story="German tanks raced through the Ardennes to the Channel in May 1940, trapping the British Expeditionary Force and French armies against the sea. On 24 May the German advance paused outside Dunkirk. From 26 May to 4 June the Royal Navy and a fleet of 'little ships' took over 338,000 men off the beaches and the mole. The army left its equipment behind.",
 why="German tanks had cut the Allied armies in half. The halt order, the RAF over the beaches and the French rearguard bought the days the evacuation needed.",
 then="France surrendered three weeks later. Britain had an army, with almost no heavy equipment, and Churchill warned that wars are not won by evacuations.",
 fact="The 'little ships' included Thames pleasure boats, fishing smacks and lifeboats; about 700 took part."),
"w2-norway": dict(
 bluf="Germany set out to secure Swedish iron ore shipped through Narvik and to win naval bases on the Atlantic before Britain could mine the route. It took Denmark in hours and Norway in two months in April to June 1940, at the cost of half its destroyer fleet. The botched Allied response brought down British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain and put Winston Churchill in power on the day Germany attacked France.",
 now="Norway's wartime experience ended its neutrality: it was a founding member of NATO in 1949.",
 story="Germany invaded Denmark and Norway on 9 April 1940. Denmark surrendered within hours. Norwegian King Haakon VII refused to accept a Nazi puppet government and British and French troops landed at Narvik and in central Norway, then withdrew as France fell; Norway capitulated on 10 June. The Commons debate on the failure brought down Chamberlain, and Churchill became prime minister on 10 May 1940.",
 why="Swedish iron ore shipped through the Norwegian port of Narvik was vital to German industry, and Norway's coast offered bases for submarines and surface ships against Britain. Britain was about to mine Norwegian waters.",
 then="Churchill took over the same day Germany attacked France. Norway's government-in-exile and merchant fleet joined the Allied war effort.",
 fact="Norwegian Nazi Vidkun Quisling seized the radio to declare himself prime minister on the day of the invasion; his name became the word for a collaborator."),
"eu194196": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler invaded the Soviet Union to win living space in the east, destroy 'Jewish Bolshevism' and take Ukraine's grain and the Caucasus's oil. The largest invasion in history reached the outskirts of Moscow by December 1941 and was stopped there. The war became a four-year contest that killed about 27 million Soviet citizens and three-quarters of Germany's military dead, and the Red Army's march to the Elbe fixed the division of Europe for forty-five years.",
 now="The invasion turned the war into the largest in history, cost the Soviet Union about 27 million dead, and brought the Red Army to the Elbe, which decided the division of Europe for forty-five years.",
 story="On 22 June 1941 over three million Axis troops invaded the USSR on a front from the Baltic to the Black Sea. They encircled Soviet armies at Minsk, Smolensk and Kyiv, reached the outskirts of Moscow and besieged Leningrad, and were stopped by Soviet counterattacks in December. Behind the lines SS killing squads murdered Jews and others in mass shootings.",
 why="Hitler's goals of 'living space' in the east and the destruction of 'Jewish Bolshevism' came first. The grain of Ukraine and the oil of the Caucasus were the material prize.",
 then="After Stalingrad in 1942-43 and Kursk in 1943 the Red Army pushed west to Berlin. The USSR lost about 27 million people, and about three-quarters of German military deaths came on the Eastern Front.",
 fact="Russia remembers it as the Great Patriotic War; Victory Day is its largest holiday and the memory is used to justify policy, including the war in Ukraine."),
"w2-declareus": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler declared war on the United States four days after Pearl Harbor, though no treaty required it, in the belief that war with America was coming anyway and Japan would tie it down in the Pacific. The declaration let President Franklin Roosevelt commit America's economy to Europe first. Germany now faced the United States, Britain and the USSR together, and its defeat became a matter of time.",
 now="It brought the world's largest economy into the war against Germany without Roosevelt having to ask Congress for it, and let the Allies put Germany's defeat before Japan's.",
 story="Four days after Pearl Harbor, on 11 December 1941, German Chancellor Adolf Hitler declared war on the United States in a speech to the Reichstag, and Italian leader Benito Mussolini followed. Germany's alliance with Japan was defensive and did not require it, and Japan had not consulted Germany before attacking. Hitler held that the US was already waging an undeclared naval war in the Atlantic and that Japan would tie it down in the Pacific.",
 why="Hitler underestimated American power and judged that war was coming anyway. The declaration freed his submarines to attack American shipping.",
 then="Roosevelt adopted a 'Germany first' strategy. US troops were fighting in North Africa by November 1942.",
 fact="German submarines sank hundreds of ships off the American coast in the first half of 1942 while American cities kept their lights on."),
"w2-leningrad": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler chose to starve Leningrad rather than storm it, intending to erase the city of Lenin. The siege ran 872 days from September 1941 to January 1944 and killed about a million civilians, most of them from hunger in the first winter. The city held, and its survival became the central symbol of Soviet endurance and of Russian war memory since.",
 now="The siege is central to Russia's memory of the war, and St Petersburg marks the end of the blockade every 27 January.",
 story="German Army Group North cut the last land route to Leningrad on 8 September 1941 and Hitler ordered the city starved rather than stormed. In the first winter the bread ration fell to 125 grams a day and people ate wallpaper paste and pets. Supplies came over the frozen Lake Ladoga, the 'Road of Life'. The siege ended on 27 January 1944.",
 why="Hitler wanted to wipe out the city of Lenin and chose starvation over the cost of street fighting. Finland's advance from the north closed the ring.",
 then="The siege lasted until January 1944. About a million civilians died, most of them from starvation.",
 fact="Dmitri Shostakovich's Seventh Symphony was performed in the besieged city in August 1942 by an orchestra of survivors, broadcast by loudspeaker toward the German lines."),
"w2-stalingrad": dict(
 bluf="Germany set out to take Stalingrad to cover its drive for the Caucasus oil and to hold a city that bore Stalin's name. The Red Army broke through the Romanian flanks in November 1942, trapped about 300,000 men of the German Sixth Army and starved them into surrender by 2 February 1943. Germany lost an army and the initiative in the east, and its allies began looking for a way out of the war.",
 now="Stalingrad destroyed a German army and ended the Wehrmacht's offensive capacity in the east; from February 1943 Germany was retreating, and the Soviet claim to have carried the war rests on it.",
 story="German forces under General Friedrich Paulus reached Stalingrad in August 1942 and fought for it street by street and building by building. On 19 November the Soviets launched Operation Uranus, smashing through the Romanian armies on the flanks and encircling about 300,000 men of the German Sixth Army. Hitler forbade a breakout; supplied by air with a fraction of what it needed, the army starved, and its last troops surrendered on 2 February 1943. About 91,000 were taken prisoner, and only a few thousand ever returned home.",
 why="Stalingrad guarded the route to the oil of the Caucasus and bore Stalin's name. Hitler's refusal to let the Sixth Army withdraw turned a defeat into its destruction.",
 then="Germany's last big offensive in the east failed at Kursk in July 1943. Romania, Hungary and Italy began looking for a way out of the war.",
 fact="Hitler promoted Paulus to field marshal the day before he surrendered, on the reasoning that no German field marshal had ever been taken alive; Paulus surrendered anyway."),
"w2-wannsee": dict(
 bluf="SS General Reinhard Heydrich called fifteen senior officials to a villa at Wannsee to put every ministry behind the murder of Europe's Jews, which the Nazi leadership had already decided on. In ninety minutes they settled the division of labour for deporting 11 million people to the east. The Holocaust ran from then on as a state project of administrators, and the minutes are the clearest surviving record of it.",
 now="Wannsee turned the murder of Europe's Jews from an SS operation into a programme of the whole state, which is why the Holocaust is read as a crime of administrators as much as of fanatics.",
 story="Mass shootings in the occupied USSR had already killed hundreds of thousands of Jews by the end of 1941. On 20 January 1942 SS General Reinhard Heydrich chaired a meeting of fifteen officials from the SS, the party and the ministries at a villa on the Wannsee in Berlin, with SS Lieutenant Colonel Adolf Eichmann taking the minutes. Heydrich set out the 'Final Solution': deporting Jews from across Europe to the east, where those fit for work would be worked to death and the rest killed.",
 why="The Nazi leadership had already decided to exterminate Europe's Jews. Wannsee coordinated the government agencies involved and settled the SS's authority over them.",
 then="Deportations to the death camps accelerated across Europe through 1942. By 1945 about six million Jews had been murdered.",
 fact="One copy of the minutes, out of thirty, survived in a Foreign Office file and was found by American prosecutors in 1947."),
"w2-italy": dict(
 bluf="The Allies invaded Sicily to knock Italy out of the war and tie down German troops while the invasion of France waited. About 160,000 US, British and Canadian troops took the island in 38 days, Mussolini fell within two weeks, and Italy surrendered in September. Germany then occupied the peninsula and turned it into a front that cost the Allies twenty months of fighting and delayed nothing on the German side.",
 now="The Italian campaign dragged on until May 1945, a lesson in how costly it is to fight up a narrow, mountainous peninsula against a determined defender.",
 story="On 10 July 1943 about 160,000 US, British and Canadian troops under American General Dwight Eisenhower landed in Sicily. They took the island in 38 days. German forces escaped across the Strait of Messina with their equipment.",
 why="British Prime Minister Winston Churchill called Italy the 'soft underbelly' of Axis Europe. Knocking Italy out of the war would tie down German troops while the Allies prepared the invasion of France.",
 then="Mussolini fell two weeks after the landings. The Allies landed on the Italian mainland in September 1943 and fought up the peninsula until May 1945.",
 fact="General George Patton slapped two shell-shocked soldiers in Sicilian field hospitals and was removed from command for nearly a year."),
"w2-italy-b": dict(
 bluf="Italy's Fascist Grand Council and King Victor Emmanuel III removed Benito Mussolini to get Italy out of a war it was losing. Italy surrendered in September 1943, Germany occupied the north and centre and set Mussolini up as a puppet, and the country became a battleground and a civil war at once. A dictatorship had collapsed from inside when defeat loomed, and the resistance that followed became the founding myth of the Italian Republic.",
 now="Italy marks 25 April as Liberation Day, and the anti-fascist resistance is a founding myth of the Italian Republic.",
 story="On 25 July 1943, two weeks after the Allied landing in Sicily, the Fascist Grand Council voted against Italian leader Benito Mussolini and King Victor Emmanuel III had him arrested. Marshal Pietro Badoglio's government surrendered on 8 September. Germany occupied the north and centre, freed Mussolini from a mountain hotel and set him up as ruler of a puppet republic at Salò.",
 why="Military defeat, Allied bombing of Italian cities and the invasion of Sicily convinced Fascist leaders and the king that Mussolini had to go.",
 then="Italy fought a civil war alongside the Allied campaign. Partisans shot Mussolini on 28 April 1945 and hung his body in a Milan square.",
 fact="Mussolini was freed from the Gran Sasso by German glider troops under Otto Skorzeny in a raid that cost no lives on either side."),
"w2-kursk": dict(
 bluf="Germany set out to regain the initiative after Stalingrad by pinching off the Soviet bulge around Kursk with its new Tiger and Panther tanks. Forewarned, the Red Army built defences many kilometres deep, stopped the attack within a week and counterattacked. Germany had launched its last major offensive in the east, and the Red Army held the initiative for the rest of the war.",
 now="Kursk ended Germany's capacity to attack in the east; from July 1943 the Red Army chose where and when to fight, which carried it to Berlin in under two years.",
 story="On 5 July 1943 Germany launched Operation Citadel, a pincer attack on a Soviet bulge in the front around Kursk, with new Tiger and Panther tanks. Forewarned by intelligence, the Soviets under Marshal Georgy Zhukov had built belts of minefields and anti-tank defences many kilometres deep and massed reserves behind them. The German attack stalled within a week; Hitler called it off as the Allies landed in Sicily, and Soviet counteroffensives retook Orel, Kharkiv and Kyiv by November.",
 why="Hitler wanted to regain the initiative after Stalingrad and shorten the front. He delayed the attack by two months to wait for new tanks, which gave the Soviets time to prepare.",
 then="The Soviets advanced steadily west and held the initiative for the rest of the war. Germany shifted troops to Italy after the Allied landing in Sicily.",
 myth="Prokhorovka on 12 July was the largest tank battle in history and a Soviet victory. The Soviet tank army lost several times as many tanks as the Germans there; the battle was won by the defensive belts and the reserves, over weeks."),
"w2-tehran": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Joseph Stalin wanted a firm date for a second front in France; British Prime Minister Winston Churchill wanted to keep operating in the Mediterranean; US President Franklin Roosevelt wanted Soviet help against Japan and a postwar order. Roosevelt sided with Stalin, and the three committed to invade France in May 1944 and agreed in principle to move Poland's borders west. The bargains that shaped postwar Europe began here, over the heads of the Poles.",
 now="It began the great-power bargains that shaped postwar Europe, including Poland's new borders, decided over the heads of the Poles.",
 story="US President Franklin Roosevelt, British Prime Minister Winston Churchill and Soviet leader Joseph Stalin met together for the first time in Tehran, from 28 November to 1 December 1943. Stalin, whose armies had borne most of the fighting, pressed for a second front in France; Churchill preferred operations in the Mediterranean, and Roosevelt sided with Stalin. They committed to invade France in May 1944 and agreed in principle to move Poland's borders west.",
 why="The Allies needed to coordinate the final defeat of Germany. Roosevelt wanted Soviet entry into the war against Japan and Stalin's support for a postwar United Nations.",
 then="D-Day was set for spring 1944. Stalin agreed to join the war against Japan after Germany's defeat.",
 fact="Roosevelt stayed in the Soviet embassy compound after reports of a German plot; Soviet listening devices recorded his conversations."),
"w2-warsawghetto": dict(
 bluf="The Jewish fighters of the Warsaw ghetto, under Mordechai Anielewicz, chose to fight the final deportation to Treblinka and die on their own terms rather than in the gas chambers. Several hundred lightly armed fighters held the SS for nearly a month from 19 April 1943 before the ghetto was burned block by block. The largest Jewish armed revolt of the Holocaust became the central symbol of resistance against annihilation in Israeli and Polish memory.",
 now="It became a lasting symbol of Jewish resistance and dignity in the face of annihilation, central to Israeli and Polish memory of the Holocaust.",
 story="Most of the ghetto's people had already been sent to Treblinka in the summer of 1942. When the SS entered to clear the remaining 50,000 or so on 19 April 1943, several hundred fighters of the Jewish Combat Organisation under Mordechai Anielewicz and the Jewish Military Union opened fire. SS General Jürgen Stroop burned the ghetto block by block. Resistance ended on 16 May 1943 when Stroop blew up the Great Synagogue.",
 why="Facing deportation to Treblinka, the fighters chose to resist and die fighting.",
 then="The SS destroyed the ghetto, and its survivors were sent to death camps. The uprising inspired revolts at Treblinka and Sobibor later that year.",
 fact="Anielewicz died in the command bunker at Mila 18 on 8 May 1943, aged 24; Stroop was hanged in Warsaw in 1952."),
"eu194497": dict(
 bluf="The Western Allies set out to open the second front in France that Stalin had demanded since 1941 and to reach central Europe before the Red Army. About 156,000 troops landed on five Normandy beaches on 6 June 1944 at a cost of about 10,000 casualties on the first day and held. Western Europe was liberated by the Western Allies rather than the Soviets, and the line where the armies met became the Cold War border.",
 now="The line where Western and Soviet armies met became the Iron Curtain; D-Day anniversaries remain major moments of Western alliance remembrance.",
 story="On 6 June 1944, after months of deception about where they would land, about 156,000 American, British, Canadian and other Allied troops under American General Dwight Eisenhower crossed the Channel to five Normandy beaches, supported by airborne troops and thousands of ships. They suffered about 10,000 casualties on the first day. The beachhead held and a million men were ashore within a month.",
 why="Stalin had demanded a second front since 1941. The Western Allies needed to defeat Germany and reach central Europe before the Red Army did.",
 then="Paris was freed in August 1944. Germany was squeezed between the Western Allies and the Red Army until its surrender in May 1945.",
 fact="The Allies used a fake army with inflatable tanks to convince Germany the main landing would come at Calais."),
"w2-bagration": dict(
 bluf="The Red Army set out to destroy German Army Group Centre in Belarus and reach Poland, timing the blow to follow D-Day. In five weeks from 22 June 1944 it advanced about 500 km and destroyed some 28 German divisions, a worse defeat for Germany than Stalingrad. The Red Army stood on the Vistula and the border of East Prussia, in position to settle eastern Europe's future by occupation.",
 now="Bagration put the Red Army in Poland and decided that eastern Europe would be Soviet-occupied; it is overlooked in the West next to D-Day though it destroyed more German divisions, and the gap in memory still shapes politics between Russia and the West.",
 story="On 22 June 1944, three years to the day after Germany's invasion, the Red Army attacked German Army Group Centre in Belarus. Deceived about where the blow would fall and forbidden by Hitler to retreat, German forces were encircled at Vitebsk, Bobruisk and Minsk. In five weeks the Soviets advanced about 500 km toward the Vistula and East Prussia, destroying some 28 German divisions, a worse defeat for Germany than Stalingrad.",
 why="The Soviets aimed to drive Germany out of the USSR and reach Poland. They timed the offensive to follow D-Day, when German reserves were committed in the west.",
 then="Poland's Home Army rose in Warsaw on 1 August 1944, hoping to free the city before the Soviets arrived. The Red Army halted on the Vistula until January 1945.",
 fact="Some 57,000 German prisoners from Bagration were marched through Moscow on 17 July 1944, followed by water trucks washing the streets."),
"w2-bulge": dict(
 bluf="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler launched his last reserves through the Ardennes to split the British and Americans, take Antwerp and force a negotiated peace in the west. Surprise carried the attack 100 km in a week, then US troops held at Bastogne, the weather cleared and Allied air power returned. Germany had spent its last armour, and the Allies crossed the Rhine in March 1945 against an army with nothing left.",
 now="It shows how surprise can briefly overturn a lost strategic position and cannot reverse it; the reserves spent in the Ardennes were the ones Germany lacked on the Rhine and the Oder.",
 story="On 16 December 1944 Germany attacked through the Ardennes in fog and snow, as it had in 1940, with about 200,000 men against a thinly held American sector. The advance made a bulge 100 km deep in the Allied line. US troops held the road junction at Bastogne until the weather cleared on 23 December and Allied aircraft returned. By late January 1945 the line was back where it had started, and the largest battle fought by the US Army in the war had cost it about 75,000 casualties.",
 why="Hitler hoped a surprise attack would split the British and Americans, take the port of Antwerp and force a negotiated peace in the west.",
 then="Allied armies crossed the Rhine in March 1945 and Germany collapsed; its last armoured reserves had been spent in the Ardennes.",
 fact="Asked to surrender at Bastogne, US General Anthony McAuliffe replied with one word, 'Nuts', which the German envoys had to have explained."),
"w2-warsaw44": dict(
 bluf="The Polish Home Army rose to free Warsaw before the Red Army arrived and give the government-in-exile in London a claim to rule Poland. The Soviets halted across the Vistula while German forces crushed the rising over 63 days, killed between 150,000 and 200,000 civilians and razed the city. Poland's chance of a non-communist government died with it, and the memory fixed Polish distrust of Russia.",
 now="Warsaw stops for a minute every 1 August, a sign of how central the uprising is to Polish identity and its distrust of Russia.",
 story="On 1 August 1944 the Polish Home Army, loyal to the government-in-exile in London, rose in Warsaw as the Red Army approached. The Soviets halted across the Vistula, and Soviet leader Joseph Stalin at first refused to let Western supply planes use Soviet airfields. After 63 days the insurgents surrendered on 2 October; between 150,000 and 200,000 civilians had been killed, and Hitler ordered the city razed.",
 why="The Home Army aimed to liberate Warsaw and give Poland's government-in-exile a claim to rule. Stalin had no interest in a Polish government he did not control.",
 then="The Soviets took the ruined city in January 1945 and installed a communist government. Home Army veterans were persecuted under it for decades.",
 fact="By the time the Red Army entered, about 85 per cent of Warsaw's buildings had been destroyed, much of it by demolition squads after the surrender."),
"eu194598": dict(
 bluf="The Allies had demanded unconditional surrender since 1943 so that no German could claim, as after 1918, that the army had been undefeated. With Berlin taken and Hitler dead, the German High Command signed at Reims on 7 May 1945 and again in Berlin on 8 May. The Nazi regime ended with the state itself, the four victors occupied and divided Germany, and the division of Europe between them followed within four years.",
 now="8 May is marked across Europe as Victory in Europe Day, and the total defeat it marked is why Germany, unlike after 1918, had no stab-in-the-back myth and rebuilt as a democracy that confronts its crimes.",
 story="German Chancellor Adolf Hitler killed himself in his Berlin bunker on 30 April 1945 as Soviet troops fought through the city. His successor, Grand Admiral Karl Dönitz, sought a surrender to the Western Allies alone and was refused. General Alfred Jodl signed the unconditional surrender at Reims on 7 May, and Field Marshal Wilhelm Keitel signed again in Berlin on 8 May at Soviet insistence.",
 why="Allied armies had overrun Germany from east and west, Berlin had fallen to the Soviets, and Hitler was dead.",
 then="The Allies divided Germany into four occupation zones and tried Nazi leaders at Nuremberg. Some 12 million Germans were expelled from eastern Europe, and Germany was split into East and West in 1949.",
 fact="Because the Berlin signing came after midnight Moscow time, Russia marks Victory Day on 9 May, a day after the West."),
"w2-auschwitz": dict(
 bluf="The Red Army reached Auschwitz on 27 January 1945 in the course of its advance into Poland and found about 7,000 prisoners the SS had left behind. The camp, the largest Nazi killing centre, had murdered about 1.1 million people, about a million of them Jews. Its records, survivors and the site itself became the evidence on which the Nuremberg trials, the Genocide Convention and the law of crimes against humanity rest.",
 now="The camp's liberation produced the evidence, in survivors, records and the site itself, on which the Nuremberg trials, the Genocide Convention and the legal definition of crimes against humanity rest.",
 story="Soviet troops reached Auschwitz on 27 January 1945 and found about 7,000 sick and starving prisoners. Days earlier the SS had forced some 58,000 others west on 'death marches' and blown up the gas chambers to hide the evidence. At Auschwitz, the largest Nazi killing centre, about 1.1 million people had been murdered, about a million of them Jews.",
 why="The Soviet advance reached the camp, which the SS had largely evacuated.",
 then="American and British troops liberated Buchenwald, Bergen-Belsen and Dachau in April 1945, with press and film crews. The evidence was used at the Nuremberg trials.",
 fact="27 January is International Holocaust Remembrance Day; the site is visited by millions."),
"w2-dresden": dict(
 bluf="Allied commanders bombed Dresden to wreck transport behind the Eastern Front and support the Soviet advance, and Britain's Bomber Command held that area bombing would break German morale. About 1,300 bombers over 13-15 February 1945 made a firestorm that destroyed the old city and killed up to 25,000 people. The attack became the standing test case for the morality of bombing cities, and its inflated death toll a tool of propaganda from Goebbels to the present far right.",
 now="Dresden remains the most debated Allied action of the war, and the argument over whether area bombing was effective or justified has shaped the law and practice of targeting since.",
 story="Over 13-15 February 1945 about 1,300 British and American heavy bombers attacked Dresden, a rail and industrial hub crowded with refugees fleeing the Soviet advance. Incendiary bombs created a firestorm that destroyed the old city. A commission appointed by the city put the death toll at up to 25,000 in 2010; Nazi propaganda inflated it to 200,000, and inflated figures still circulate.",
 why="Allied commanders wanted to disrupt German transport behind the Eastern Front and support the Soviet advance. Britain's Bomber Command under Air Chief Marshal Arthur Harris held that area bombing would break German morale.",
 then="British Prime Minister Winston Churchill questioned the bombing policy in a memorandum weeks later. The attack fed postwar debate over whether area bombing was effective or justified, and far-right groups exploit it by inflating the toll.",
 fact="American novelist Kurt Vonnegut survived the raid as a prisoner of war in a meat locker, which gave 'Slaughterhouse-Five' its title."),
"w2-nuremberg-trials": dict(
 bluf="The Allies set out to punish the Nazi leaders by law rather than by summary execution, to document their crimes for history, and to establish that waging aggressive war and mass murder were crimes. The International Military Tribunal tried 22 leaders from November 1945, sentenced twelve to death and acquitted three. Leaders could now be held personally responsible for crimes against peace and humanity, the foundation of international criminal law.",
 now="It is the foundation of international criminal law, and the International Criminal Court of 2002 builds on it.",
 story="From November 1945 the International Military Tribunal, with judges from the US, Britain, France and the USSR, tried 22 leading Nazis for conspiracy, crimes against peace, war crimes and the new charge of crimes against humanity. Using the regime's own documents and film from the camps, American chief prosecutor Robert Jackson and his colleagues laid out the Holocaust in court. Twelve were sentenced to death on 1 October 1946, Reichsmarschall Hermann Göring killed himself before he could be hanged, and three were acquitted.",
 why="The Allies wanted to punish the Nazi leaders, document their crimes for history, and establish that waging aggressive war and mass murder were crimes in law. Churchill had favoured shooting the leaders; the Americans insisted on a trial.",
 then="The UN adopted the Genocide Convention in 1948. The tribunals for Yugoslavia and Rwanda in the 1990s and the International Criminal Court followed its model.",
 myth="Nuremberg was simply victors' justice. Only the losers were tried, and the Soviet judges sat over crimes their own state had committed; the tribunal still acquitted three defendants and rested its verdicts on the regime's own documents."),
"w2-potsdam": dict(
 bluf="The three victors met at Potsdam to settle the peace in Europe and finish the war against Japan. US President Harry Truman, Soviet leader Joseph Stalin and British Prime Minister Winston Churchill, replaced mid-conference by Clement Attlee, confirmed Germany's division into zones, moved Poland's border west to the Oder and Neisse and approved the expulsion of millions of Germans from eastern Europe. Germany's and Poland's borders were fixed for good, and the distrust between Washington and Moscow was already visible.",
 now="Poland's and Germany's border today was set here, along with the expulsion of millions of Germans from eastern Europe.",
 story="US President Harry Truman, Soviet leader Joseph Stalin and British Prime Minister Winston Churchill met at Potsdam from 17 July to 2 August 1945. Churchill was replaced by Clement Attlee on 28 July after losing the British election. They confirmed Germany's division into four zones, moved Poland's western border to the Oder and Neisse and approved the 'orderly and humane' transfer of Germans from Poland, Czechoslovakia and Hungary. Truman told Stalin of a powerful new weapon.",
 why="The victors needed to settle the peace in Europe and finish the war against Japan. Roosevelt had died and Churchill was replaced mid-conference, which left less trust at the table.",
 then="Atomic bombs fell on Japan on 6 and 9 August 1945. The wartime alliance gave way to the Cold War within two years.",
 fact="Stalin showed no surprise at Truman's news of the bomb; Soviet spies had already told him about it."),
"w2-yalta": dict(
 bluf="US President Franklin Roosevelt, British Prime Minister Winston Churchill and Soviet leader Joseph Stalin met at Yalta to settle Germany's defeat and the peace with Soviet armies already across eastern Europe. They agreed on occupation zones, the United Nations, Soviet entry into the war against Japan and 'free and unfettered elections' in Poland. The elections never took place, Stalin installed communist governments across the region, and 'Yalta' became the word for great powers dividing other people's lands.",
 now="'Yalta' is shorthand in eastern Europe for being sold out by the great powers, and is invoked whenever outsiders discuss peace terms over the heads of those affected, as with Ukraine.",
 story="US President Franklin Roosevelt, British Prime Minister Winston Churchill and Soviet leader Joseph Stalin met at Yalta in Crimea from 4 to 11 February 1945. They agreed to divide Germany into occupation zones, with one for France; to hold 'free and unfettered elections' in Poland under a government built around the Soviet-backed one; to found the United Nations; and that the USSR would join the war against Japan within three months of Germany's defeat, in return for territory in East Asia.",
 why="The three needed to plan Germany's defeat and the peace while the war was ending. Soviet armies were already in eastern Europe, which left the Western leaders little to bargain with.",
 then="Stalin installed communist governments across eastern Europe, and the promised free elections in Poland never took place. Roosevelt died two months after the conference.",
 myth="Roosevelt gave eastern Europe away at Yalta. The Red Army already held it; the agreement recorded promises the West had no means to enforce."),
"na194769": dict(
 bluf="US President Harry Truman set out to take over Britain's role of holding Greece and Turkey against communist pressure after London said it could no longer pay. He asked Congress in March 1947 for $400 million and a commitment to support free peoples resisting subjugation, and got both. Containment became the core of US strategy for forty years, and the United States replaced Britain as guarantor of the Western order.",
 now="It began the era of US commitments to defend other countries, the basis of the American alliance system that is still debated today.",
 story="In February 1947 Britain told Washington it could no longer support Greece, where a civil war was running, or Turkey, under Soviet pressure over the Straits. On 12 March US President Harry Truman asked Congress for $400 million in aid and said the United States must support free peoples resisting subjugation by armed minorities or outside pressures. Congress approved in May.",
 why="Britain could no longer afford to support Greece and Turkey against communist pressure, and Washington feared Soviet expansion into the eastern Mediterranean.",
 then="Containment became the core of US Cold War strategy for 40 years. The Marshall Plan followed in 1947 and NATO in 1949.",
 fact="Senator Arthur Vandenberg told Truman that to get the money he would have to 'scare hell out of the American people'; the speech did."),
}
